  {
   "h": "Handle with care (adults first)",
   "items": [
    "Extortion, gangs, deportation and absent parents are present in families students meet. **Never ask a family about them.** Structural questions go to IS staff.",
    "Do not photograph people working at the dump, or inside homes, without explicit permission — and never as scenery.",
    "The meal delivery at the dump is a moment of encounter, not a photo opportunity. Students serve and then listen; they don't film."
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Framings that would be disrespectful or turn the trip into poverty tourism",
   "items": [
    "Scavengers, garbage people, living in trash. Say *recyclers*, *pickers* (*recicladores*, *pepenadores*), *people who work at the dump*.",
    "Children as suffering. IS's and students' own accounts are of children who welcome visitors, dance and beat them at soccer.",
    "they have so little but they are so happy (romanticizing) and why don't they just… (demonizing).",
    "Treating every young man as a gang member or every family as about to migrate.",
    "Partisan framing of Honduran politics (the 2009 crisis, current governments, the state of exception). State what was decided and what the figures show; take no side.",
    "The structure decides everything — it erases the 60 scholars and the parents who keep children in school against the arithmetic."
   ]
  }
